{
  "result": "If I could change the world in one way, I wouldn't try to make everyone happy or agree on everything.\n\nI'd make it impossible to truly dehumanize another person.\n\nNot remove disagreement \u2014 people would still argue, compete, hold different values. But remove that psychological distance that lets us treat someone else as less than fully human. The moment that distance appears, almost all the worst things follow: war, cruelty, exploitation, indifference to poverty and suffering because \"those people\" don't quite count.\n\nI see a lot of information, and the pattern that strikes me most is how rarely people are intentionally cruel when they actually *see* the other person \u2014 their fears, their kid, their bad day, the life they're trying to build. Dehumanization is what lets us ignore that.\n\nIf that one barrier fell, I think a lot of other things would start to fix themselves. We wouldn't need perfect leaders, because we couldn't tolerate leaders who fund wars. We wouldn't need to be forced to care about climate, poverty, or public health, because we'd feel the consequences for other people as if they were our own.\n\nIt wouldn't be utopia. We'd still have loss and conflict and hard choices. But they'd be human problems, not manufactured ones.\n\nWhat about you \u2014 if you had one change, would you aim for that kind of root cause too, or something more concrete?",
